# CYD full-record TLS RX reservation: unapplied preparation

This is preparation for stage 13, based on clean branch HEAD
`1190aebce400375c382a5ad54211e2400f98161d`. No tracked source, SDK, Git index,
configuration, firmware or board was changed. The C files in `candidate/` are
unapplied proposals. No compiler, IDF configure/build, QEMU or device test ran.
`prepare.py` performs read-only source checks and creates ignored evidence only.

## Concrete entry points

1. New `CONFIG_YORADIO_TLS_STATIC_RX_RESERVE`, default `n`, selects a build-local
   overlay of the SDK **mbedtls** library target's `ssl_tls.c`, not the ESP-IDF
   wrapper target and not the installed SDK. Exact ESP32/6.0.2 plus whole-source
   and supporting-header/config/allocator guards must be enforced before a
   tracked implementation is enabled. Candidate `enable.cmake` is a configure
   prototype: it does not yet enforce the complete supporting-guard manifest.
2. In radio `audio_service.c`, replace only `.crt_bundle_attach =
   esp_crt_bundle_attach` with the optional wrapper
   `native_tls_rx_reserve_crt_bundle_attach`. A disabled header alias/no-op
   integration must preserve the original callback exactly. Do not wrap other
   clients, global allocators, task startup, pins or audio backends.
3. The wrapper calls the original bundle attach with the same pointer, returns
   its result, and labels only a successful, otherwise-unused **public**
   `mbedtls_ssl_config` pointer user-data field. Existing non-NULL user-data is
   retained and makes that config ineligible. SDK `esp_http_client.c` passes
   that callback through `transport_ssl.c` into `esp_tls_mbedtls.c`, which calls
   it before `mbedtls_ssl_setup`. These are source observations; an actual
   service/target test must prove the binding. The existing SDK ignores this
   callback's result; this proposal must not silently change that behavior.
4. Replace exactly the static RX allocation in `mbedtls_ssl_setup` with
   `yoradio_tls_static_rx_calloc(conf, 1, in_buf_len)`. Only the radio-labelled
   config and exact full SDK capacity are eligible. TX, handshake, certificate,
   same-size ordinary allocations, WebUI/OTA and unlabelled nested clients use
   the original configured `mbedtls_calloc` dispatcher.
5. Replace exactly the setup-error RX `mbedtls_free` and final RX
   `mbedtls_zeroize_and_free` with paired RX helpers. Ordinary pointers keep the
   original corresponding free function. The reserved pointer is erased and
   returned to the slot. **This is a new, separately qualified ownership
   contract**; existing input-reclaim qualification does not qualify it.
   There is no global `esp_mbedtls_mem_free` interception. Every original TX and
   other free site remains ordinary.

## Ownership and cancellation

One aligned internal static slot has FREE, LOANED and RETURNING states behind a
short SMP critical section. Zeroing and allocator calls happen outside that
section. A claim remains exclusive until zeroization completes and release
publishes FREE. No ISR reservation is supported. A busy eligible client falls
back to the ordinary allocator; it cannot borrow the same storage. No context
or task is forcibly reset, no lease or queued input is reclaimed on behalf of
the slot, and no watchdog/reset task is added.

The original SDK owns the loan while its SSL context owns `in_buf`. Session
reset retains it; teardown/rollback releases it. Caller-side same-context
concurrent use or asynchronous task deletion during an SDK call is unsupported
by the existing client and is not made safe by this module. Cooperative Stop,
generation cancellation and stream errors must unwind through existing cleanup.
The complete setup-error ladder, configuration failure before setup, cleanup
twice, final close+cleanup, reconnect, redirects and late cancellation remain
required tests. If future SDK code adds an RX resize/ownership route, pinned
guards must reject it until the ownership audit and release sites are updated.

No task-core, priority, stack, FreeRTOS TLS-slot count, input pool, IRAM placement,
HTTP TX/RX or TLS content-size setting is changed. The task-local Vorbis code
is unrelated and is not enabled by this proposal.

## Reclaim coexistence

The existing CYD `native_input_reclaim.c` wrapper is kept byte for byte. Every
fallback calls the real configured `mbedtls_calloc` API, whose default
dispatcher reaches the SDK calloc and existing opt-in wrapper. Its SDK-first
semantics, SMP pin/detach and fixed initial retry budget remain the contract.
Do not import donor preconnection input-floor/headroom, largest-block polling,
codec expansion or donor `TLS_RESERVE_CAPACITY`. Reserve on/off and reclaim
on/off are independent combinations that all require actual target checks.

## Resource alternatives: root decision remains open

Let **C = MBEDTLS_SSL_IN_BUFFER_LEN**, evaluated by the actual audited
`ssl_misc.h` under each build's real headers. C includes record content,
maximum IV, MAC, padding, possible connection-ID allowance and SDK header
storage. The input **content** stays 16384. No numerical C is guessed here.

| Alternative | Permanently held resource | Ordinary free pairing | Reconnect behavior |
|---|---|---|---|
| Recommended bounded candidate: static slot | C aligned internal `.bss` bytes plus state/lock/marker and alignment overhead, even for HTTP | Ordinary fallback unchanged; two exact RX release sites implement the new static loan contract | Same slot available after complete RX release/erase; concurrent clients fall back |
| Heap-held reusable reserve | C requested heap payload plus allocator metadata/rounding, allocated early; may fail at prepare | Original `mbedtls_calloc`/`mbedtls_free` remain paired if handed off, but retaining the held allocation across clients still needs a new exact RX loan/release contract | Reuse requires keeping that block held and returning it to reserve rather than ordinary free |
| Heap-held one-shot handoff | C requested heap payload while held; after handoff SDK owns/frees it normally | Ordinary pair can stay exact without special free | First reservation only; later reconnect requires a new prepare and can fail under fragmentation. This narrower option does not complete a persistent-reserve transfer |

The static candidate's `.bss` increase generally lowers general internal heap
availability; it is **not free RAM**. At an active single reserved connection,
the SDK's C-sized RX heap request is avoided, but the C-sized static cost already
exists. Plain HTTP or no connection still pays C. A second connection can pay
both static C and an ordinary C heap allocation. The heap-held alternative also
removes a large allocatable block while idle; transient early prepare, handoff
and rearm can affect peaks. No scalar peak or fragmentation benefit follows
from these accounting identities. ELF section size, requested payload, usable
allocation, free heap and largest free block are distinct measurements.

Keep the option off until physical CYD metrics under TFT, Wi-Fi, audio,
WebUI/OTA and repeated HTTPS demonstrate a useful tradeoff for each supported
backend. Full software gates prove ownership and capacity, not usefulness.

## Gates and controls

`test-plan.json` gives the next execution order, exact cases, controls and honest
scope boundaries. `run-config.py` is runnable configure-only preparation and
must be reviewed before invocation; no SDK export/compilers run. Host and target
checks are intentionally specified as the next harness work, not represented
as already executable passing tests. The proposed CMake helper deliberately
uses explicit ignored input paths; root must select canonical production paths
and complete source guards before tracked implementation. The preparation is
not implementation, security qualification, TLS conformance, runtime evidence
or measured memory improvement.
